//? Title: Binomial, hypergeometric and gamma quantiles
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

A discrete quantile is the first integer at which the cumulative probability
reaches the target. The binomial models independent trials. The
hypergeometric models draws without replacement, so its smallest possible
count can be positive.

The gamma law has a continuous positive support. Supplying the upper-tail
probability directly permits targets too small to recover by subtracting a
lower-tail probability from one.

```{cpp, id=distribution-quantiles, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/distributions.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::random;
    std::println("| Count distribution | Cumulative probability | Quantile |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto level : {.5, .95}) {
        std::println("| Binomial, 10 trials | {:.2f} | {} |", level, binomial_quantile(level, 10, .3));
        std::println("| Hypergeometric, 5 draws | {:.2f} | {} |",
                     level, hypergeometric_quantile(level, 20, 7, 5));
    }
    const auto quantile = gamma_quantile(1e-100, 3, 2, false);
    std::println("\nGamma with shape 3 and scale 2 has upper-tail quantile {:.6f}. "
                 "The probability above this point is {:.2e}.",
                 quantile, gamma_cdf(quantile, 3, 2, false));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Count distribution | Cumulative probability | Quantile |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Binomial, 10 trials | 0.50 | 3 |
| Hypergeometric, 5 draws | 0.50 | 2 |
| Binomial, 10 trials | 0.95 | 5 |
| Hypergeometric, 5 draws | 0.95 | 3 |

Gamma with shape 3 and scale 2 has upper-tail quantile 481.078890. The probability above this point is 1.00e-100.
// result_end

> Count successes with unequal probabilities

The Poisson-binomial distribution keeps a separate probability for each
independent Bernoulli trial. Convolving the trial probabilities also handles
deterministic trials. In this example one success is certain.

```{cpp, id=poisson-binomial, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/poisson_binomial.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const datamunge::random::PoissonBinomial count({1, .1, .4, .9});
    std::println("| Successes | Probability |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: |");
    for (unsigned k = 1; k <= 4; ++k)
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} |", k, count.pmf(k));
    std::println("\nThe 95th percentile is {} successes.", count.quantile(.95));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Successes | Probability |
| ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.054 |
| 2 | 0.528 |
| 3 | 0.382 |
| 4 | 0.036 |

The 95th percentile is 3 successes.
// result_end

> Allow extra variation in the count

The negative binomial allows a count variance greater than its mean. Its
precision parameter controls the size of the extra variance. The beta-binomial
keeps the count within the trial range and gives each set of trials a shared
success probability drawn from a beta distribution.

```{cpp, id=overdispersed-counts, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/count_distributions.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::random;
    std::println("| Distribution | Count | Mass | Upper tail | 90th percentile |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Negative binomial, mean 7.3 and precision 2.5 | 8 | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {} |",
                 negative_binomial_pmf(8, 7.3, 2.5), negative_binomial_cdf(8, 7.3, 2.5, false),
                 negative_binomial_quantile(.90, 7.3, 2.5));
    std::println("| Beta-binomial, 27 trials and shapes 0.3 and 2.5 | 6 | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {} |",
                 beta_binomial_pmf(6, 27, .3, 2.5), beta_binomial_cdf(6, 27, .3, 2.5, false),
                 beta_binomial_quantile(.90, 27, .3, 2.5));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Distribution | Count | Mass | Upper tail | 90th percentile |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Negative binomial, mean 7.3 and precision 2.5 | 8 | 0.0659 | 0.3345 | 14 |
| Beta-binomial, 27 trials and shapes 0.3 and 2.5 | 6 | 0.0300 | 0.1618 | 9 |
// result_end
